(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-4-appendicular-skeleton-quiz-version-4"]={"title":"The Appendicular Skeleton — Anatomy Exam 4 — Set 4","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"How many bones are in each free upper limb, not counting the pectoral girdle?","choices":["27 bones","32 bones","30 bones","14 bones"],"answer":2,"exp":"Each free upper limb has 30 bones: 1 humerus, 1 radius, 1 ulna, 8 carpals, 5 metacarpals, and 14 phalanges. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Which end of the clavicle is generally described as more rounded, and which is flatter?","choices":["The sternal end is round; the acromial end is flat","The sternal end is flat; the acromial end is round","Both ends are equally flattened in shape","Both ends are equally rounded in shape"],"answer":0,"exp":"The medial (sternal) end of the clavicle is more rounded, while the lateral (acromial) end is flattened where it meets the acromion. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Which small notch on the superior border of the scapula transmits the suprascapular nerve?","choices":["The spinoglenoid notch of the scapula","The suprascapular (scapular) notch","The radial notch of the ulna","The trochlear notch of the ulna"],"answer":1,"exp":"The suprascapular notch on the superior border of the scapula transmits the suprascapular nerve beneath the transverse scapular ligament. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"Which two rounded projections mark the distal end of the humerus at the elbow, on either side of the joint?","choices":["The greater and lesser tubercles","The medial and lateral malleoli","The medial and lateral epicondyles","The greater and lesser trochanters"],"answer":2,"exp":"The medial and lateral epicondyles are the two projections flanking the distal humerus and serve as muscle-attachment sites at the elbow. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"The intertubercular sulcus (bicipital groove) of the humerus houses the tendon of which muscle?","choices":["The lateral head of the triceps brachii","The middle fibers of the deltoid","The supraspinatus of the rotator cuff","The long head of the biceps brachii"],"answer":3,"exp":"The intertubercular sulcus lodges the tendon of the long head of the biceps brachii as it runs along the proximal humerus. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"Which end of the ulna bears the head of the ulna, and which bears the olecranon?","choices":["The head is distal; the olecranon is proximal","The head is proximal; the olecranon is distal","Both are at the proximal end","Both are at the distal end"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ulna has its olecranon at the proximal end (the elbow) and its small rounded head at the distal end (near the wrist). — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"The proximal head of the radius articulates with the capitulum and is encircled by which ligament?","choices":["The patellar ligament","The anular ligament","The interosseous membrane","The transverse scapular ligament"],"answer":1,"exp":"The head of the radius is held against the radial notch of the ulna by the anular ligament, which encircles it and allows rotation. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"When the forearm is pronated, what is the position of the radius relative to the ulna?","choices":["The radius lies parallel to the ulna","The radius separates far from the ulna","The radius crosses over the ulna","The radius rotates the palm to face forward"],"answer":2,"exp":"In pronation the distal radius swings medially and crosses over the ulna, turning the palm posteriorly. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 2"},{"q":"Which carpal bone of the distal row is the largest and sits centrally in the wrist?","choices":["The pisiform","The trapezium","The lunate","The capitate"],"answer":3,"exp":"The capitate is the largest carpal bone and occupies a central position in the distal row of the wrist. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"In the mnemonic 'Sally left the party to take Cathy home,' which carpal does the first word ('Sally') represent?","choices":["The scaphoid","The pisiform","The hamate","The trapezoid"],"answer":0,"exp":"In the mnemonic the first word, 'Sally,' stands for the scaphoid, the first bone of the proximal row on the lateral side. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"Which bones together form the bony pelvis?","choices":["The two hip bones, the sacrum, and the coccyx","The two femurs and the sacrum","The two hip bones and the lumbar vertebrae","The sacrum, coccyx, and the two patellae"],"answer":0,"exp":"The bony pelvis is formed by the two hip (coxal) bones anteriorly and laterally with the sacrum and coccyx posteriorly. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Which anterior projection of the ilium is an easily palpable landmark at the front of the hip?","choices":["The posterior superior iliac spine","The anterior superior iliac spine","The ischial tuberosity","The pubic symphysis"],"answer":1,"exp":"The anterior superior iliac spine is the readily palpable bony point at the front of the iliac crest, a common surface landmark of the hip. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"The greater and lesser sciatic notches are separated by which bony projection?","choices":["The iliac crest","The pubic tubercle","The ischial spine","The anterior inferior iliac spine"],"answer":2,"exp":"The ischial spine projects between the greater sciatic notch above it and the lesser sciatic notch below it. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 2"},{"q":"Which large articular surface on the medial ilium meets the sacrum at the sacroiliac joint?","choices":["The iliac fossa","The acetabular fossa","The pubic crest","The auricular surface"],"answer":3,"exp":"The ear-shaped auricular surface on the medial ilium articulates with the matching surface of the sacrum at the sacroiliac joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"The femur articulates with the tibia at the knee through which paired distal structures?","choices":["The medial and lateral condyles","The greater and lesser trochanters","The medial and lateral malleoli","The head and neck of the femur"],"answer":0,"exp":"The medial and lateral condyles of the distal femur articulate with the corresponding condyles of the tibia at the knee joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Which structure connects the greater and lesser trochanters on the posterior femur?","choices":["The intertrochanteric line","The intertrochanteric crest","The linea aspera","The gluteal tuberosity"],"answer":1,"exp":"The intertrochanteric crest is the ridge that joins the greater and lesser trochanters on the posterior surface of the proximal femur. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"Within the tendon of which muscle group is the patella embedded?","choices":["The hamstrings","The gastrocnemius","The quadriceps femoris","The adductor group"],"answer":2,"exp":"The patella is a sesamoid bone embedded within the tendon of the quadriceps femoris on the anterior knee. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 2"},{"q":"In anatomical position, which of the two leg bones is the medial, weight-bearing bone?","choices":["The fibula","The patella","The talus","The tibia"],"answer":3,"exp":"The tibia is the larger medial bone of the leg and bears essentially all the body's weight transmitted to the ankle. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Which distal tibial projection forms the prominence on the medial side of the ankle?","choices":["The medial malleolus","The lateral malleolus","The tibial tuberosity","The intercondylar eminence"],"answer":0,"exp":"The medial malleolus is the distal tibial projection that forms the bony prominence on the medial side of the ankle. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Which distal fibular projection forms the prominence on the lateral side of the ankle?","choices":["The medial malleolus","The lateral malleolus","The head of the fibula","The tibial plateau"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lateral malleolus is the distal end of the fibula and forms the bony prominence on the lateral side of the ankle. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"How many tarsal bones are in each foot?","choices":["8 tarsal bones","5 tarsal bones","7 tarsal bones","14 tarsal bones"],"answer":2,"exp":"Each foot has 7 tarsal bones forming the posterior half of the foot, including the talus and calcaneus. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 2"},{"q":"Which tarsal bone forms the heel and is the largest bone of the foot?","choices":["The talus","The navicular","The cuboid","The calcaneus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The calcaneus is the largest tarsal bone and forms the heel of the foot, transmitting body weight to the ground. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Which tarsal bone articulates superiorly with the tibia and fibula to help form the ankle joint?","choices":["The talus","The calcaneus","The cuboid","The cuneiform"],"answer":0,"exp":"The talus is the tarsal bone that articulates with the tibia and fibula superiorly, forming the ankle (talocrural) joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Which row of hand bones lies between the carpals and the phalanges, forming the palm?","choices":["The tarsals","The metacarpals","The cuneiforms","The sesamoids"],"answer":1,"exp":"The five metacarpals lie between the carpal bones of the wrist and the phalanges of the fingers, forming the palm. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Through the greater sciatic notch (foramen) passes which major nerve to the lower limb?","choices":["The suprascapular nerve","The phrenic nerve","The sciatic nerve","The radial nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"The sciatic nerve exits the pelvis through the greater sciatic notch/foramen to supply much of the lower limb. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 2"},{"q":"Besides the pelvis, which general feature can the skeleton reveal about an individual, per the material?","choices":["Blood type","Eye color","Native language","Approximate age"],"answer":3,"exp":"The material notes that skeletal features can indicate characteristics such as age, in addition to sex and body size. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"The roughened deltoid tuberosity on the humeral shaft serves as the attachment site for which muscle?","choices":["The deltoid muscle","The biceps brachii","The pectoralis minor","The trapezius muscle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The deltoid tuberosity is a roughened area on the lateral humeral shaft where the deltoid muscle attaches. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Which large opening in the hip bone is bounded by the ischium and pubis and is largely closed by a membrane in life?","choices":["The greater sciatic foramen","The obturator foramen","The vertebral foramen","The optic canal"],"answer":1,"exp":"The obturator foramen is the large opening framed by the ischium and pubis, mostly covered by the obturator membrane in life. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"Which proximal tibial ridge, seen between the condyles, is called the intercondylar eminence?","choices":["The distal projection at the inner ankle","The anterior ridge for the patellar ligament","The upward bony spine between the tibial condyles","The lateral prominence at the outer ankle"],"answer":2,"exp":"The intercondylar eminence is the raised bony ridge (spine) between the medial and lateral condyles on the superior tibial surface. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 2"},{"q":"Which distal posterior notch of the femur separates the medial and lateral condyles?","choices":["The greater trochanter","The head of the femur","The lateral malleolus","The intercondylar fossa"],"answer":3,"exp":"The intercondylar fossa is the deep posterior notch of the distal femur that separates the medial and lateral condyles. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"}]};
